\\) for all \\( x \\) in the interval \\([-1, 1]\\), the second condition is always satisfied within the given domain. Therefore, the domain of \\( f(x) \\) is \\([-1, 1]\\).\n\n### Step 2: Simplify the expression using trigonometric substitution\nLet \\( x = \\cos \\theta \\), where \\( \\theta \\in [0, \\pi] \\). Then, \\( \\sqrt{1 - x^2} = \\sin \\theta \\). Substituting these into the function, we get:\n\\[\nf(\\cos \\theta) = \\sqrt{58 - 42 \\cos \\theta} + \\sqrt{149 - 140 \\sin \\theta}.\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Evaluate \\( f(x) \\) at critical points\nWe will evaluate \\( f(x) \\) at the endpoints of the interval \\([-1, 1]\\):\n- When \\( x = -1 \\):\n  \\[\n  f(-1) = \\sqrt{58 - 42(-1)} + \\sqrt{149 - 140 \\sqrt{1 - (-1)^2}} = \\sqrt{58 + 42} + \\sqrt{149 - 140 \\cdot 0} = \\sqrt{100} + \\sqrt{149} = 10 + \\sqrt{149}.\n  \\]\n\n- When \\( x = 1 \\):\n  \\[\n  f(1) = \\sqrt{58 - 42 \\cdot 1} + \\sqrt{149 - 140 \\sqrt{1 - 1^2}} = \\sqrt{58 - 42} + \\sqrt{149 - 140 \\cdot 0} = \\sqrt{16} + \\sqrt{149} = 4 + \\sqrt{149}.\n  \\]\n\n### Step 4: Compare the values\nWe have:\n\\[\nf(-1) = 10 + \\sqrt{149}\n\\]\n\\[\nf(1) = 4 + \\sqrt{149}\n\\]\n\nSince \\( 4 + \\sqrt{149} < 10 + \\sqrt{149} \\), the minimum value of \\( f(x) \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{4 + \\sqrt{149}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{4 + \\sqrt{149}}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
